Subject: [PATCH 55/55] drivers/pci: publish the expansion ROM, bind the 1275 config-space words

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

B.3 Spike 3's original subject -- a PCI option ROM's header read from REAL device
memory -- was kept as a named UNCOVERED row because "this firmware binds no
config-space or port-I/O words" (the poke-engine review's did-not-prove list,
2026-08-30). Measured 2026-09-03, the row was closer than the plan said:
ob_pci_configure_bar() already allocates every device's expansion ROM base
register like a BAR and SETS ITS ENABLE BIT (`reg == 6`), so under this firmware
an option ROM is mapped and readable -- QEMU's `info pci` showed BAR6 assigned at
0x41120000 and `xp` read the 0x55AA header there -- and then nothing said where:
pci_set_reg() and pci_set_assigned_addresses() stop at the BARs, so a node's
assigned-addresses carried BAR0/BAR1 only. Two things, both small:

1. THE ROM BASE REGISTER IS PUBLISHED. The 1275 PCI bus binding (2.2.1.1 "reg",
   4.1.2 "assigned-addresses") lists it -- 0x30, or 0x38 in a bridge header --
   beside the BARs. The ROM slot is config->{regions,assigned,sizes}[num_bars],
   one past the BARs; its low bit is ENABLE, not I/O, so it goes through its own
   encoder (pci_encode_rom_entry: 32-bit non-prefetchable memory, 11-bit mask)
   rather than pci_decode_pci_addr(). The property buffers grow to hold a seventh
   entry (32 and 38 cells had no room for one -- 7 x 5 = 35, and reg carries the
   config-space entry too).

2. config-{b,w,l}@ ( config-addr -- data ) and config-{b,w,l}! ( data config-addr
   -- ), config-addr in the phys.hi form bus<<16 | dev<<11 | fn<<8 | reg -- the
   first cell of a node's own "reg" property plus the register. The binding
   places them as PCI bus-node methods an FCode driver reaches through my-space;
   this firmware's FCode table has no entry for them and nothing bound any
   config-space (or port-I/O) word on any arch, so they are GLOBAL words -- the
   seam a Forth reader can actually reach, and named as such. They go through
   the same pci_config_read{8,16,32}/write the enumerator uses. Each arch calls
   ob_pci_bind_config_words() from arch_init AFTER device_end(): a bind_func in
   ob_pci_init() would land before the dictionary is searchable -- the exact
   shape of patches 14/16 -- which is why the call sits beside platform-boot.

What it makes possible, measured on x86 AND amd64 (the `optrom` track):
dsl/optrom.fth finds the ROM from assigned-addresses, checks the SAME address in
config space (id=100e8086, rom=41040001 -- enable bit set), reads the PCI ROM
header and PCIR through the device-register backend at the live BAR (sig aa55,
"PCIR", vendor/device ffff, code type 1 = Open Firmware), byte-loads the FCode
straight out of the ROM into the node -- which the card's program then renames
to `fcode-card` and stamps `fcode-marker = FCODE-FROM-CARD-RAN`, the OFW lab's
outcome on the rival firmware -- and, through config-l!, clears the enable bit
and watches the header vanish at the same address, then brings it back. ppc:
enumerates and publishes the entry and answers config-l@ (Arch-tested); the
ROM read itself there needs pci-map-in (a bus address on mac99), not done.

Two upstream quirks noticed and left alone, each named where it lives: a
bridge's ROM (reg index 2) never gets its enable bit (`reg == 6` is the test),
and vga_config_cb's ppc path writes the ROM BAR value to PCI_COMMAND.

